<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE topic SYSTEM "https://resources.jetbrains.com/writerside/1.0/xhtml-entities.dtd">
<topic xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
       xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation="https://resources.jetbrains.com/writerside/1.0/topic.v2.xsd"
       id="maven-goals" title="Maven goals">

    <show-structure for="chapter,procedure" depth="2"/>

    <tldr>
        <p>The pom block that adds <code>aether-weaver-maven-plugin</code> is on
            <a href="install.topic">Install</a>.</p>
    </tldr>

    <p>
        <code>aether-weaver-maven-plugin</code> declares four goals. Three bind to a phase of
        their own; <code>audit</code> binds to none and is invoked directly.
    </p>

    <table style="header-row">
        <tr><td>Goal</td><td>Binds to</td><td>What one run does</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>stubs</code></td><td><code>generate-sources</code></td>
            <td>Writes extension stubs</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>weave</code></td><td><code>process-classes</code></td>
            <td>Rewrites your own classes</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>weave-tests</code></td><td><code>process-test-classes</code></td>
            <td>Rewrites your test classes</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>audit</code></td><td>nothing; you invoke it</td>
            <td>Prints what a jar records</td></tr>
    </table>

    <p>
        A default of <code>true</code> or <code>false</code> marks a <code>boolean</code>
        parameter. The rest are a <code>File</code>. A path default sits under the build
        directory. Every goal but <code>audit</code> also takes a read-only
        <code>project</code>. It is always the current one.
    </p>

    <chapter title="What the two weaving goals share" id="shared">
        <p>
            <code>weave</code> and <code>weave-tests</code> extend one base class, so they take
            the same four parameters. <code>stubs</code> shares <code>skip</code> with them;
            <code>audit</code> takes none of them.
        </p>

        <table style="header-row">
            <tr><td>Parameter</td><td>Property</td><td>Default</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>skip</code></td><td><code>aether.weaver.skip</code></td>
                <td><code>false</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>failOnError</code></td><td><code>aether.weaver.failOnError</code></td>
                <td><code>true</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>explain</code></td><td><code>aether.weaver.explain</code></td>
                <td><code>false</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>dumpDirectory</code></td><td><code>aether.weaver.dump</code></td>
                <td>none</td></tr>
        </table>

        <list>
            <li><p>Both goals apply the weaves that <code>META-INF/aether/weaves.json</code> names
                in the directory they rewrite, and read extension declarations off the classpath
                — <a href="manifest-format.topic">The weave manifest</a>.</p></li>
            <li><p><code>stubs</code> reads <code>aether.weaver.skip</code> as well, so setting it
                on the command line stops that goal too. <code>audit</code> never reads
                it.</p></li>
            <li><p><code>failOnError</code> decides only whether a collected diagnostic of error
                severity fails the build; clear it and the error is still logged, and the woven
                classes are still written. An unresolved classpath or a class file that cannot be
                read or written fails the goal regardless of it.</p></li>
            <li><p><code>explain</code> prints the report at info level after the weaving, so it
                names what each injection point matched —
                <a href="diagnosing-a-weave.topic">Diagnosing a weave</a>.</p></li>
            <li><p><code>dumpDirectory</code> takes the original bytes, the woven bytes and a diff
                for each class of the directory it rewrites that the run changed. A dump it cannot
                write is <code>AW4090</code>: check that the directory is writable.</p></li>
        </list>

        <note>
            <p>
                No goal declares a <code>verification</code> parameter, so the engine's strict
                default stands for all four —
                <a href="diagnostics-verification.topic">AW4xxx</a> has what a refusal looks like.
            </p>
        </note>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="weave" id="weave">
        <p>
            Rewrites your output directory in place. It resolves the compile and runtime
            scopes.
        </p>

        <table style="header-row">
            <tr><td>Parameter</td><td>Property</td><td>Default</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>weaveDependencies</code></td>
                <td><code>aether.weaver.weaveDependencies</code></td>
                <td><code>false</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>allowSigned</code></td><td><code>aether.weaver.allowSigned</code></td>
                <td><code>false</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>dependencyOutputDirectory</code></td><td>none</td>
                <td><code>aether-weaver/dependencies</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>classesDirectory</code></td><td>read-only</td>
                <td>the output directory</td></tr>
        </table>

        <list>
            <li><p>Left off, <code>weaveDependencies</code> keeps every dependency as its
                publisher shipped it. A weave whose target lives in one then stays planned and
                unwoven, and the run warns how many targets it never found.</p></li>
            <li><p>Switched on, every resolved jar goes through the same weaver in path order,
                and only the classes the plan changed are written out. The jars themselves are
                never modified — <a href="build-time-weaving.topic#dependencies">Weaving dependencies</a>.</p></li>
            <li><p>Nothing puts that output directory on a classpath. <code>AW2501</code> lists
                what was written: put the directory ahead of the jars wherever your build
                assembles one.</p></li>
            <li><p><code>allowSigned</code> has no effect unless <code>weaveDependencies</code> is
                also set. With both set, a signed dependency jar that would otherwise be refused as
                <code>AW3002</code> is woven and reported as <code>AW3020</code> instead. Both codes
                are specific to a dependency jar; your own module's classes are never signed —
                <a href="diagnostics-policy.topic">AW3xxx</a> has the codes.</p></li>
            <li><p><code>dependencyOutputDirectory</code> names no property, so you set it in the
                plugin's own <code>configuration</code> element rather than on the command
                line.</p></li>
        </list>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="weave-tests" id="weave-tests">
        <p>
            Rewrites your test output directory. It resolves the test scope. It writes nothing
            else: dependency weaving belongs to <code>weave</code> alone.
        </p>

        <table style="header-row">
            <tr><td>Parameter</td><td>Property</td><td>Default</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>testClassesDirectory</code></td><td>read-only</td>
                <td>the test output directory</td></tr>
        </table>

        <p>
            Its other four parameters are the shared ones above. The weaves it applies come
            from that directory alone — <a href="testing-woven-code.topic">Testing woven code</a>.
        </p>

        <warning>
            <p>
                Your own main classes reach the test classpath as a separate entry, and this goal
                counts only the test output and your declared dependencies as direct. Weaves you
                declared in your main sources are therefore reported as <code>AW3010</code> on
                every test build, and none of its offered remedies fits a module reporting
                against itself — <a href="diagnostics-policy.topic">AW3xxx</a> has why there is
                nothing to do about it.
            </p>
        </warning>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="stubs" id="stubs">
        <p>
            Writes a copy of every extended receiver that is found and has something left to add.
            Each carries the members contributed to it, so your calls compile.
            <a href="extension-methods.topic">Extension methods</a> has the model.
        </p>

        <table style="header-row">
            <tr><td>Parameter</td><td>Property</td><td>Default</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>skip</code></td><td><code>aether.weaver.skip</code></td>
                <td><code>false</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>outputDirectory</code></td><td>none</td>
                <td><code>aether-weaver/stubs</code></td></tr>
        </table>

        <list>
            <li><p>A receiver the running JVM's runtime image holds goes under
                <code>patch/</code>, one directory per module; one it does not hold goes to
                <code>classpath/</code>.</p></li>
            <li><p>The goal prints the <code>--patch-module</code> arguments at info level and
                configures nothing. Pass them to the compiler yourself, or the build compiles
                against the unpatched receivers.</p></li>
            <li><p>No diagnostic fails this goal. An error is logged at error level and every
                other severity at warning level, with the code and the message alone; the details
                and the remedy are dropped.</p></li>
            <li><p>A declaration refused as <code>AW1308</code> or <code>AW1309</code> is logged
                and then not stubbed. Check both of them in
                <a href="diagnostics-declaration.topic">AW1xxx</a>, which carries the
                remedies.</p></li>
            <li><p>Three things do fail it: an unresolved compile classpath, a stub it cannot
                write, and a missing receiver. A receiver is missing when a required extension
                names it and it is on neither the compile classpath nor the runtime image. Add
                the dependency that provides it, or declare that extension
                <code>@Extension(require = Require.OPTIONAL)</code>.</p></li>
        </list>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="audit" id="audit">
        <p>
            Reads a jar, a war or a directory of classes. It prints what each woven class
            records about itself. It names no phase and declares
            <code>requiresProject = false</code>. You invoke it directly, inside a project or
            outside one.
        </p>

        <table style="header-row">
            <tr><td>Parameter</td><td>Property</td><td>Default</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>artifact</code></td><td><code>aether.weaver.artifact</code></td>
                <td>the output directory</td></tr>
        </table>

        <code-block lang="console"><![CDATA[
$ mvn %group%:aether-weaver-maven-plugin:%version%:audit \
      -Daether.weaver.artifact=target/app.jar
[INFO] fixture/Target.class
[INFO]   ← fixture.Greeting                INJECT   onGreet()V  →  greet()
[INFO]
[INFO] 1 class, 1 modification, fingerprint <64 hex characters>, no policy overrides
]]></code-block>

        <list>
            <li><p>One line per woven class, one indented line per modification it records, then a
                summary. A class carrying no record is left out, and an artefact carrying none is
                a single line.</p></li>
            <li><p>Each fingerprint is a plan's whole digest, and the summary joins the
                distinct ones with <code>+</code> —
                <a href="determinism.topic">Determinism</a> says what moves one.</p></li>
            <li><p>The summary closes with <code>no policy overrides</code>, or with
                <code>A POLICY OVERRIDE WAS USED</code> where an audited class records
                one.</p></li>
            <li><p>It writes nothing, re-weaves nothing and raises no diagnostic. Name the
                artefact yourself where the current project has no output to read, or the goal
                fails saying so.</p></li>
        </list>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="Binding all three build goals" id="binding">
        <p>
            One execution carries all three. Each goal takes its own phase. A
            <code>configuration</code> element beside the executions applies to each.
        </p>

        <code-block lang="xml"><![CDATA[
<plugin>
    <groupId>%group%</groupId>
    <artifactId>aether-weaver-maven-plugin</artifactId>
    <version>%version%</version>
    <configuration>
        <weaveDependencies>true</weaveDependencies>
    </configuration>
    <executions>
        <execution>
            <goals>
                <goal>stubs</goal>
                <goal>weave</goal>
                <goal>weave-tests</goal>
            </goals>
        </execution>
    </executions>
</plugin>
]]></code-block>
    </chapter>

